The staff's financial stability assessment noted that valuation pressures in some asset markets were elevated while vulnerabilities from leverage at nonbank financial institutions remained notable. Bank funding was stable, though unrealized losses on securities holdings persisted.

Participants reviewed bank lending standards, which the Senior Loan Officer Opinion Survey indicated had tightened for commercial real estate and for small business loans. Credit availability for households was little changed, and delinquencies on credit cards edged higher.

Several participants expressed concern about stress in the commercial real estate sector, particularly office properties facing refinancing needs. They noted that regional banks with concentrated exposures could face losses, which might restrain credit supply.

Given the cooling of economic activity and a continued decline in inflation, participants agreed to lower the target range for the federal funds rate by 25 basis points. They described the action as a recalibration of the policy stance in light of the balance of risks.

Participants noted that financial conditions had eased since the previous meeting, with equity prices higher and credit spreads narrower. Some warned that easier conditions could complicate the effort to restore price stability.

Members emphasized that further adjustments to the policy rate would depend on the evolution of the economic outlook, and they stated that they would continue to monitor risks to financial stability alongside incoming data.
